# arvo-format

The declared signature of a number: which values exist, and what happens to a result that is not one
of them.

A format here is not a container. It is a representable set together with the domain that set sits
in, and membership in it is one predicate rather than a family of them. You give a phase, a quantum
per magnitude and a slot range, and integers, fixed point, scaled integers and floating point all
come out as points of that one thing. Subnormals are not a special case, they fall out of taking the
maximum at the smallest magnitude, which is the test that the unification is doing real work and not
just presenting four things in one table.

Arithmetic is stated the same way throughout: an exact operation in the ambient domain, composed with
a named total adaptation back onto the representable set. The adaptation is a thing you can name and
reason about, not a flag hanging off an operation, and it has two independent coordinates.

The rounding mode says what happens between grid points, and there are six of them:
`toward_zero`, `floor`, `ceil`, `half_up`, `half_even` and `stochastic`. The word truncation is not
one of them and does not appear anywhere in this crate, because on a signed domain it names two
operations that genuinely differ, and a reader coming from the hardware and a reader coming from C
would each have been sure they knew which. Do note that dropping bits off a two's complement value is
`floor`, not `toward_zero`.

The overflow policy says what happens outside the range, and that one is open rather than fixed at
three. Wrapping, saturating and clamping are what is here; another joins by supplying what the concept
asks for.

There is no machine container anywhere in this crate and you cannot reach one from here. Every
operation is a function of the width you declared, never of whatever the value happens to sit in, and
the way that is kept true is that this crate does not depend on the crate that has containers in it.
Where the bits actually go is `arvo-placement`.

Every coordinate a format is declared with carries a type of its own, a radix, an exponent, a slot, a
phase and so on, rather than a host integer, so a numeral declared in some other crate is written in
the same types as the ones shipped here and is not a second-class member of the inventory.

`no_std`, no allocation, and everything a type knows is known at compile time.

## Status

Design phase. The documents are written and the source is being built from them, so the surface here
describes what is being implemented rather than what you can call today.
